    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters 2026-06-29 with a core of high-upside plays, led by **Corbin Carroll** (80.43) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.75). **Corbin Carroll** remains our premier offensive engine; while we factor in a minor discount for Tyler Mahle\u2019s catcher defense, the combination of a favorable park and prime #3 positioning keeps his ceiling elite. Similarly, **Mickey Moniak** presents a high-floor, high-reward profile against Sandy Alcantara. He gains significant momentum from a +14% platoon adjustment and an advantageous #2 spot in the lineup, making him the most critical UTIL play to chase both runs and stolen base opportunities. **Tyler Soderstrom** (71.20) and **Seiya Suzuki** (69.53) also project as strong starts, with **Seiya Suzuki** benefiting from a wind-aided boost at his home park and **Tyler Soderstrom** capitalizing on the favorable matchup against Eric Lauer.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision today involves the bottom of the order and our bench depth. **Cole Young** (47.47) just missed the cut, ultimately squeezed out by the more favorable park and SP skill dynamics favoring **Zach McKinstry** (49.91), who also draws a favorable base-stealing environment against Ryan Weathers. While **Jose Caballero** remains a rostered asset for his speed, his #9 projected spot and a brutal platoon disadvantage against Casey Mize kept his score down to 42.62, reinforcing why he remains on the bench despite his stolen base potential. We remain cautious regarding **Dalton Rushing**, whose score of 40.68 is the lowest in the starting lineup; the combination of a left-on-left matchup against Gage Jump and a likely #8 batting position creates a significant risk profile that barely justifies his inclusion today. Note that all projections assume lineup positioning that has yet to be finalized, and any shifts in the batting order for **James Wood** or **Henry Bolte**\u2014who both carry high scores today\u2014could shift our overall offensive output significantly.",
